# ATLAS — FUNDAMENTALS CACHE
# ==========================
# Install:  crontab -l > /tmp/ct && cat deploy/fundamentals.cron >> /tmp/ct \
#           && crontab /tmp/ct && crontab -l | tail -20
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. fundamentals.gate() is consulted per candidate at
# decision time and reads ONLY the local cache -- never the network, because the
# market-hours loop cannot afford a request per symbol per cycle. data/processed
# is gitignored, so the deploy (git reset --hard origin/main, every five minutes)
# neither delivers the cache nor destroys it. It has to be built here, and until
# it is, gate() FAILS CLOSED and blocks every symbol. That is correct and it is
# also invisible unless something says so, which is why the loop reports it as
# BLOCKED_NO_FUNDAMENTALS and the 15:30 report leads with it.
#
# EVERYTHING FINISHES BEFORE 09:20 IST, when the loop starts. The box runs UTC,
# so IST = UTC + 5:30 and these are written in UTC.
#
# THE TWO FETCHES RUN AT DIFFERENT CADENCES, because their sources do:
#
#   announcements   auditor resignations, forensic audits, OFS and QIP filings
#                   appear any day. DAILY, and announcements ONLY: a full
#                   --refetch re-downloads two SHP XBRL documents per symbol,
#                   about half a gigabyte across the universe, to re-read
#                   promoter and pledge numbers that come from a quarterly
#                   filing and cannot have moved.
#
#   shareholding    promoter % and pledge, quarterly filings. WEEKLY is already
#                   four times faster than the data changes, and it is the run
#                   that admits symbols new to the store.
#
#   annual results  balance sheet and cash flow. WEEKLY, and generous: NSE's
#                   period=Annual endpoint stops at FY2024 for every symbol, so
#                   there is nothing newer to find. It runs at all so that a
#                   restored source is picked up without anyone remembering to.
#
# --refresh NEVER TOUCHES THE NETWORK. It joins the two stores and writes the
# cache the gate reads, so it must run after both, and it is the only step whose
# failure leaves the gate blocking.

# ── daily: announcements, then rebuild the cache ──────────────────
# 03:00 IST — announcements for every symbol already in the store
30 21 * * * cd /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic && /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic/venv/bin/python -m engine.tier1_fetch --all --announcements-only >> /home/ubuntu/logs/tier1_announcements.log 2>&1

# 04:10 IST — join both stores into the gate cache. Runs every day even when
# neither fetch changed anything, because PARSER_VERSION may have moved with a
# deploy and a cache written by an older parser blocks the whole universe.
40 22 * * * cd /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic && /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic/venv/bin/python -m engine.fundamentals --refresh >> /home/ubuntu/logs/fundamentals_refresh.log 2>&1

# ── weekly: the full fetches ──────────────────────────────────────
# Sunday 01:30 IST — annual filings
0 20 * * 6 cd /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic && /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic/venv/bin/python -m engine.fundamentals_fetch --all >> /home/ubuntu/logs/fundamentals_annual.log 2>&1

# Sunday 02:30 IST — shareholding + announcements, full refetch
0 21 * * 6 cd /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic && /home/ubuntu/thestocklogic/venv/bin/python -m engine.tier1_fetch --all --refetch >> /home/ubuntu/logs/tier1_full.log 2>&1
